			"date": "2025-01-27T14:16:42.526Z",
			"content": "<p><span class=\"h-card\" translate=\"no\"><a href=\"https://indieweb.social/@villapirorum\" class=\"u-url mention\">@<span>villapirorum</span></a></span> truly we will have ascended to a higher plane when that is possible</p>",
			"via": "fediverse"
		},
		{
			"id": "113901189839364261",
			"parentId": "113900720218282849",
			"url": "https://front-end.social/@AmeliaBR/113901189805061181",
			"author": {
				"name": "Amelia Bellamy-Royds",
				"url": "https://front-end.social/@AmeliaBR",
				"avatarUrl": "https://cdn.masto.host/fediversezachleatcom/cache/accounts/avatars/109/304/885/279/901/783/original/ac1081e7e8ac0f19.jpg"
			},
			"date": "2025-01-27T16:10:29.000Z",
			"content": "<p><span class=\"h-card\" translate=\"no\"><a href=\"https://fediverse.zachleat.com/@zachleat\" class=\"u-url mention\" rel=\"nofollow noopener\" target=\"_blank\">@<span>zachleat</span></a></span> Yes, SVG-as-image is sandboxed (no script or references to external files) but in browsers it still uses the full web rendering engine, just with those features blocked.</p><p>The goal with the \"SVG Native\" subset was to be able to create SVG renderers that didn't need a full web engine &amp; its security/sandboxing model. The graphics would be a more direct equivalent to Android \"drawable\" vector graphics.</p><p>Use cases &amp; overview in the draft spec:<br><a href=\"https://svgwg.org/specs/svg-native/\" rel=\"nofollow noopener\" translate=\"no\" target=\"_blank\"><span class=\"invisible\">https://</span><span class=\"\">svgwg.org/specs/svg-native/</span><span class=\"invisible\"></span></a></p>",
			"via": "fediverse"
		},
		{
			"id": "113901213966570738",
			"parentId": "113901189839364261",
			"url": "https://fediverse.zachleat.com/@zachleat/113901213966570738",
			"author": {
				"name": "Zach Leatherman",
				"url": "https://fediverse.zachleat.com/@zachleat",
				"avatarUrl": "https://cdn.masto.host/fediversezachleatcom/accounts/avatars/109/286/461/031/266/152/original/dc489c3746a44e00.png"
			},
			"date": "2025-01-27T16:16:37.878Z",
			"content": "<p><span class=\"h-card\" translate=\"no\"><a href=\"https://front-end.social/@AmeliaBR\" class=\"u-url mention\">@<span>AmeliaBR</span></a></span> oh fascinating — super interested to read the CSS support crossover 👀</p>",
			"via": "fediverse"
		},
		{
			"id": "113901222949239413",
			"parentId": "113900725982506374",
			"url": "https://front-end.social/@AmeliaBR/113901222927738621",
			"author": {
				"name": "Amelia Bellamy-Royds",
				"url": "https://front-end.social/@AmeliaBR",
				"avatarUrl": "https://cdn.masto.host/fediversezachleatcom/cache/accounts/avatars/109/304/885/279/901/783/original/ac1081e7e8ac0f19.jpg"
			},
			"date": "2025-01-27T16:18:54.000Z",
			"content": "<p><span class=\"h-card\" translate=\"no\"><a href=\"https://fediverse.zachleat.com/@zachleat\" class=\"u-url mention\" rel=\"nofollow noopener\" target=\"_blank\">@<span>zachleat</span></a></span> Oh, well, if they're *designer* bananas, then maybe that's why they're $10.</p><p>(Also, belated apologies for totally braindumping on your joke thread. My other Masto server was down briefly, so I had this deep dive into grocery inflation with chart screenshots all queued up &amp; couldn't post it, then I flip over here &amp; you're joking about food inflation, &amp; hyperfocused brain was all \"wow, have I got a chart for you!\" 🍌🙃🍌)</p>",
			"via": "fediverse"
		},
		{
			"id": "113901241364449948",
			"parentId": "113901213966570738",
			"url": "https://front-end.social/@AmeliaBR/113901241339795703",
			"author": {
				"name": "Amelia Bellamy-Royds",
				"url": "https://front-end.social/@AmeliaBR",
				"avatarUrl": "https://cdn.masto.host/fediversezachleatcom/cache/accounts/avatars/109/304/885/279/901/783/original/ac1081e7e8ac0f19.jpg"
			},
			"date": "2025-01-27T16:23:35.000Z",
			"content": "<p><span class=\"h-card\" translate=\"no\"><a href=\"https://fediverse.zachleat.com/@zachleat\" class=\"u-url mention\" rel=\"nofollow noopener\" target=\"_blank\">@<span>zachleat</span></a></span> The idea was that \"SVG Native\" graphics would use CSS-compatible presentation attributes, but very limited CSS features. However, CSS variables would be allowed because of their use for palette theming in SVG-in-OpenType fonts (which were supposed to be an important use case for the standardized restricted SVG rendering environment).</p><p>Given how fast CSS has been adding new features, that section would probably need to be re-written as an allow-list instead of a block-list, though.</p>",
			"via": "fediverse"
		},
		{
			"id": "114083329961273392",
			"parentId": "113888007996670864",
			"url": "https://hachyderm.io/@mb21/114083329936958246",
			"author": {
				"name": "mb21",
				"url": "https://hachyderm.io/@mb21",
				"avatarUrl": "https://fediverse.zachleat.com/avatars/original/missing.png"
